920928244535389e-07,-2.50339189733495e-06,-3.099393507000059e-05,-0.011159623041749,-0.00024423000286333263,-0.00020168177434243262,-0.00016437610611319542,0.0,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-9.536738616588991e-07,-3.3378546504536644e-06,-0.00453586271032691,-4.589452510117553e-05,-0.0011336335446685553,0.0,-0.023471955209970474,0.0,0.0,-0.0003812778159044683,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.001793087343685329,-0.00018702188390307128,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-2.7418097943154862e-06,-1.0251946150674485e-05,0.0,-1.7523612768854946e-05,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-3.4570634852570947e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07,-2.50339189733495e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"To determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\n","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,7