\n\nThe line parameterized by\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\]lies in a plane of the form $x + y + cz = d.$  Enter the ordered pair $(c,d).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"1399635d2d6572e5d76a1cf4052c5a280e426c60e1f44ed93d39a3ce452587a8"},"seconds":30.910334825515747,"seed":144858483316681177,"stop":"eos","text":"Since the direction vector of the line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need another vector in the plane. We can use the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) itself and another vector, say \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (from the point \\((1,0,1)\\) to \\((0,0,0)\\) by shifting the origin).\n\nThe normal vector \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) to the plane is the cross product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(-3 - 0) - \\mathbf{j}(-1 + 1) + \\mathbf{k}(0 - 3) = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo, the normal vector to the plane is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe equation of the plane is then \\(x + z = d\\). To find \\(d\\), substitute the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) into the plane equation:\n\\[\n1 + 1 = d \\implies d = 2.\n\\]\nThus, the equation of the plane is \\(x + z = 2\\), which can be rewritten as \\(x + 0y + z = 2\\). Therefore, \\(c = 1\\) and \\(d = 2\\).\n\nThe ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(1, 2)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(1, 2)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSince the direction vector of the line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need another vector in the plane. We can use the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) itself and another vector, say \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (from the point \\((1,0,1)\\) to \\((0,0,0)\\) by shifting the origin).\n\nThe normal vector \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) to the plane is the cross product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(-3 - 0) - \\mathbf{j}(-1 + 1) + \\mathbf{k}(0 - 3) = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo, the normal vector to the plane is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe equation of the plane is then \\(x + z = d\\). To find \\(d\\), substitute the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) into the plane equation:\n\\[\n1 + 1 = d \\implies d = 2.\n\\]\nThus, the equation of the plane is \\(x + z = 2\\), which can be rewritten as \\(x + 0y + z = 2\\). Therefore, \\(c = 1\\) and \\(d = 2\\).\n\nThe ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(1, 2)}\\).","id":"precalculus:264"}
